<sect>trafo (Revision 1.1 -- 22/04/1998)

<p> The unit <tt/trafo/ regroups transformation routines commonly used in
geodesy. Additional transformation routines can be found in <tt/crdtrans/.

<sect1>Trafo Configuration File

<p> For many transformation routines defined in <tt/trafo/ and <tt/crdtrans/
and for all related applications a configuration file is needed. 
When installing the software, you must export the environment 
variable <tt/TRAFOCONF/ to point at this file for the routines to work.

<p> The structure of the configuration file is line-oriented. 
Every line must start with one of the following keywords: 
<tt/formtype/, <tt/frame/, <tt/rate/, <tt/ellipsoid/, or <tt/geoid/. 
Blank lines or lines starting with <tt/#/ are ignored. 

<sect2> formtype

<p> <tt/formtype/ associates an abbreviation <tt/identifier/ to a representation
of a set of 3 dimesional coordinates. The syntax is:
<p>
<tscreen>
<bf/formtype/ identifier coord1 coord2 coord3
</tscreen>

<p> where <tt/identifier/ is an arbitrarly chosen name for the coordinate type. Possible
coordinates types are: <tt/x, y, z, n, e, h, lat, lon, rlat, rlon, glat, glon, dmslat,
dmslon/.
<p> with:
<p><tt/x, y, z/: geocentric coordinates
<p><tt/n, e, h/: Northing Easting height
<p><tt/lat, lon/: latitude longitude (in degree)
<p><tt/rlat, rlon/: latitude longitude (in radian)
<p><tt/glat, glon/: latitude longitude (in grad)
<p><tt/dmslat, dmslon/:  latitude longitude (in deg:min:sec)


<p>Examples of <tt/formtype/ definitions from configuration file:
<tscreen><verb>
formtype xyz x y z
formtype llh lat lon h
formtype swissyxh e n h
</verb></tscreen>

<sect2>frame

<p> <tt/frame/ defines a 7-parameter transformation between two cartesian
reference frames. 

<tscreen>
<bf/frame/ source target dx dy dz scale angletype alpha beta gamma epoch
</tscreen>

<p> where <tt/source/ is the name of a reference frame 1,  
<tt/target/ is the name of  a reference frame 2. 
The 7-parameter transformation is given by 3 translations 
<tt/dx, dy/ and <tt/dz/ (in meters), a scale factor <tt/scale/ (in ppm) and
3 rotations: <tt/alpha/ (around the x-axis), <tt/beta/ (around the y-axis) and
<tt/gamma/ (around the z-axis). The unit of all three rotation angles is defined
by the field <tt/angletype/, which must be one of the following 3 items: 
<tt/deg/ for degree, <tt/''/ for arcseconds, or <tt/rad/ for radians. The last
parameter <tt/epoch/ defines the reference epoch of  the transformation set
 and is given in years (decimal). It is used in connection with  <tt/rate/.
Applying this transformation to the coordinates of a point given in reference
frame 1 yields its coordinates with respect to reference frame 2. 

P2 = HIER FEHLT NOCH EINE FORMEL !!!!

Examples of <tt/frame/ definitions from configuration file:
<tscreen><verb>
frame ch1903+ etrf 674.374 15.056 405.346 0.0 deg 0.0 0.0 0.0 0.0
frame itrf94 itrf92 0.008 0.002 -0.008 -0.0008 cc 0.0 0.0 0.0 0.0
frame etrf wgs84 0.055 -0.541 -0.185 -0.014 cc 0.057 -0.001 0.022 0.0
</verb></tscreen>

<sect2>rate

<p> <tt/rate/ defines the temporal change of a 7 parameter transformation 
as defined by <tt/frame/. The parameters are given in the corresponding unit
per year.

<tscreen>
<bf/rate/  source target dx-dot dy-dot dz-dot scale-dot angletype alpha-dot beta-dot gamma-dot
</tscreen>

<p> If <tt/rate/ is defined, they are used in conjunction with <tt/frame/
to calculate linearly the transformation set for the required epoch.
 <tt/Pi(t) = Pi(t0) + Pi-dot*(t-t0)/
<p>where t0 corresponds to the field <tt/epoch/ of <tt/frame/
<tt/Pi(t0)/ represents one of the 7 parameters from <tt/frame/, 
<tt/Pi-dot/ its corresponding rate from <tt/rate/. <tt/t/ is the required epoch. 

<p>
Example of <tt/rate/ definition from configuration file:
<tscreen><verb>
frame itrf93 etrf 0.019 0.053 -0.021 0.0 cc 0.0 0.0 0.0 1989.0
rate itrf93 etrf 0.0 0.0 0.0 0.0 '' -0.00032 -0.00078 0.00067
</verb></tscreen>

<sect2>ellipsoid

<p> The next keyword <tt/ellipsoid/ allows to define ellipoid parameter in the
following simple way.

<tscreen>
<bf/ellipsoid/ identifier  major-axis  excentricity_squared
</tscreen>

<p> Examples of <tt/ellipsoid/ definitions from configuration file:
<tscreen><verb>
ellipsoid sphere1 1.000 0.0
ellipsoid sphere 6378137.000 0.0
ellipsoid wgs84 6378137.000 0.00669438
ellipsoid grs80 6378137.000 0.00669438002290079
ellipsoid bessel 6377397.155 0.006674372230614
</verb></tscreen>

<sect2>geoid

<p> For the conversion of ellipsoidal heights to geoid heights, grid files
containing the geoid undulation can be defined using the keyword <tt/geoid/:

<tscreen>
<bf/geoid/ identifier coord1 coord2 gridformat (filename || filenumber)
</tscreen>

<p> 
<tt/coord1/ and <tt/coord2/ define the type of the x and y coordinates of the
grid mesh. <tt/gridformat/ indicates the type of grid. At the moment
the following 2 types are supported ( <tt/dsaa/ = surfer grid in ASCII
format  and <tt/gmt/ = gmt format). Depending of the format either
a <tt/filename/ for <tt/dsaa/ or a <tt/filenumber/ for <tt/gmt/ has
to be provided.

<p> Examples of <tt/geoid/ definitions from configuration file:

<tscreen><verb>
geoid gurtner e n dsaa /home/favey/project/geoid.grd
geoid world lon lat gmt 100
</verb></tscreen>


<sect1>Type definitions
<p>

<sect2>frameread and frameconv
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct frameread {
	int fwd;             /* sign for forward or reverse conversion */
	double ref_epoch;    /* reference epoch in year */
	double dx[3];	     /* translation in [m] */
	double scale;	     /* scale - 1 in [ppm] */
	double rot[3];	     /* small rotation elements [rad] */
	double ddx[3];       /* rates of translation in [m/year] */
	double dscale;	     /* rates of scale in [ppm/year] */
	double drot[3];      /* rates of rotation [rad/year] */
}

struct frameconv {
	int fwd;             /* sign for forward or reverse conversion */
	double scale;        /* scale - 1 in [ppm] */
	double rm[3][3];     /* rotation matrix */
	double dx[3];        /* translation vector */
}
</verb></tscreen>

<sect2> ellipsoid
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct ellipsoid {
        double a;            /* major axis of ellipsoid in meter */
        double e2;           /* square value of excentricity */
}
</verb></tscreen>

<sect1>Routines for Parsing Configuration File

<sect2>select_formtype
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int select_formtype (const char *formtype, int ft_type[3], int ft_cols[3])
</verb></tscreen>

<p>selects <tt/formtype/ for input. (see configuration file for more information)

<sect2>select_frameconv
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct frameconv * select_frameconv (const char *source, const double inepoch, 
                                     const char *target, const double outepoch)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Selects <tt/frame/ conversion parameters from file returns pointer to 
struct or <tt/NULL/ upon failure free struct with free(3) after finishing using it.

<sect2>select_ellipsoid
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct ellipsoid * select_ellipsoid (const char *ellstr)
</verb></tscreen>

<p> Selects ellipsoid from configuration file. Returns pointer to struct or 
<tt/NULL/ upon failure. Free struct with free(3) after finishing using it.


<sect1>Routines
<p>

<sect2>frame_conv
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int frame_conv (struct frameconv *fcp, double source[3], double target[3])
</verb></tscreen>
<p> transforms the coordinates <tt/source/ from reference frame 1 to reference
frame 2, based on the transformation parameters <tt/fcp/.

<sect2>cartesian_to_spherical
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void cartesian_to_spherical(double xyz[3], 
			    double *lat, double *lon, double *radius)

</verb></tscreen>
<p> Converts cartesian coordinates <tt/xyz/ to spherical latitude (<tt/lat/),
longitude (<tt/lon/), and distance (<tt/radius/). (all angles in radians).

<sect2>spherical_to_cartesian
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void spherical_to_cartesian(double lat, double lon, double radius, 
			    double xyz[3])
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Converts cartesianspherical coordinates given by latitude (<tt/lat/),
longitude (<tt/lon/), and distance (<tt/radius/) to cartesian coordinates
(all angles in radians).


void spherical_to_cartesian(
			    double lat, 
			    double lon, 
			    double radius, 
			    double xyz[3]);

/

<sect2>xyz_to_llh
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int xyz_to_llh (const struct ellipsoid ell, const double xyz[3], 
                double *lat, double *lon, double *h);
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Converts geocentric coordinates <tt/xyz/ to ellipoidal latitude (<tt/lat/),
longitude (<tt/lon/), and ellipsoidal height (<tt/h/). The definition of 
the ellipsoidal parameters is given by <tt/ell/. (all values in meters or
radians).

<sect2>llh_to_xyz
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int llh_to_xyz (const struct ellipsoid ell, const double lat, const double lon, 
                const double h, double xyz[3]);
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Converts ellipoidal coordinates (<tt/lat/, <tt/lon/, and <tt/h/ to the
corresponding Cartesian coordinates<tt/xyz/. The definition of 
the ellipsoidal parameters is given by <tt/ell/. (all values in meters or
radians).





